## Interpretation boundaries

The project order weighs problem similarity, required-skill evidence, strength of evidence and domain/stakeholder relevance; project dates are often incomplete, so recency is not used to manufacture precision. All selections are active, registered projects with resume content. Candidate-only Power BI portfolio wording stays within visible dashboard scope. Company systems never become candidate skills by association.

Prime Street has no licence requirement in its Junior Reporting Analyst section. ESS accepts operational-data experience as an alternative to engineering/industrial experience. Morgan McKinley does not require a fixed number of years or all of Power BI, Tableau and SAP. Lidl does require a full licence and the stated degree threshold; German is optional. These distinctions materially change the recommendations.

The technical/application barriers reported here are inherited observations from the parent task: Prime Street verification challenge, ESS account gateway, Morgan McKinley automatic approval block, Lidl form untraversed. They establish neither candidate ineligibility nor successful application. Resume source/PDF/evidence mapping and both rendered A4 pages still need current QA before a reviewed PDF can be released.
